{% extends "pages/_example.jinja" %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% set example_slug = 'deployment-helper' %}
{% set example_facts = [
  ('Level', 'Intermediate'),
  ('Needs', 'Python ' ~ python_floor ~ ' or later, and tar on PATH (any POSIX system)'),
  ('Install', 'Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' from PyPI; see Getting started'),
  ('Tested', cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' on Linux, Python ' ~ python_floor ~ '; both runs repeated eight times with identical output'),
  ('Uses', 'ScopeConfig (allowlist, timeout, env_overlay, after_hooks), ExecutionContext(cwd=...), TimeoutExpired'),
] %}

{% block problem %}
<p>
  A deployment step is several commands that belong together: render a
  configuration file for the target environment, pack a release, check what
  went into it. Each runs in the same working directory, with the same
  environment, under the same deadline, and the scratch directory has to go
  away afterwards whether the step succeeded or not.
</p>
<p>
  In Cuprum the shared policy is one {{ api.ref('ScopeConfig') }}: the
  programs allowed, a default deadline, an environment overlay, and a hook
  that records every command's outcome. The working directory is a per-run
  {{ api.ref('ExecutionContext') }}, and cleanup is an ordinary
  <code>with</code> block.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block code %}
{% call ui.code_panel('deploy.py', 'Complete', foot=[verified]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

from cuprum import (
    TAR,
    ExecutionContext,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    ScopeConfig,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, TAR, name="deploy")

python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)
tar = sh.make(TAR, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

audit: list[str] = []


def record(cmd, result):
    tool = "python" if cmd.program == PYTHON else cmd.program
    audit.append(f"{tool} {cmd.argv[0]} exited {result.exit_code}")


RENDER = (
    "import os, pathlib\n"
    "pathlib.Path('app.conf').write_text(f\"env={os.environ['DEPLOY_ENV']}\\n\")"
)

policy = ScopeConfig(
    allowlist=CATALOGUE.allowlist,
    timeout=10.0,
    env_overlay={"DEPLOY_ENV": "staging"},
    after_hooks=(record,),
)

with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="deploy-") as workdir:
    staging = Path(workdir)
    here = ExecutionContext(cwd=staging)
    with scoped(policy):
        python("-c", RENDER).run_sync(context=here)
        packed = tar("-czf", "release.tgz", "app.conf").run_sync(context=here)
        listed = tar("-tzf", "release.tgz").run_sync(context=here)
    rendered = (staging / "app.conf").read_text()

print(f"rendered {rendered.strip()!r}")
print(f"packed ok={packed.ok}")
print(f"archive holds {listed.stdout.split()}")
print(f"cleaned up: {not staging.exists()}")
for line in audit:
    print(line)
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
{% endblock %}

{% block output %}
<div class="cu-pair">
  {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'terminal') %}
rendered 'env=staging'
packed ok=True
archive holds ['app.conf']
cleaned up: True
python -c exited 0
tar -czf exited 0
tar -tzf exited 0
  {% endcall %}
  <div class="cu-prose">
    <p>
      The first line comes from the file the child wrote: the child saw
      <code>DEPLOY_ENV=staging</code> from the scope's overlay without the
      parent's environment changing. The archive holds the rendered file,
      packed in the scratch directory because every command ran with that
      <code>cwd</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The directory is gone once the <code>with</code> block closes, and the
      after hook saw each of the three commands in the order they finished.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block failure %}
<div class="cu-prose">
  <p>
    Now a migration step that hangs. The scope's one-second default applies to
    every command inside it, so the step is torn down: asked to terminate,
    given <code>cancel_grace</code> to do so, then killed.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="cu-pair mt-8">
{% call ui.code_panel('deploy_timeout.py', 'Failure path', foot=[verified], flat=true) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

from cuprum import (
    ExecutionContext,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    ScopeConfig,
    TimeoutExpired,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, name="deploy")
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

MIGRATE = "import time; print('migrating', flush=True); time.sleep(30)"

policy = ScopeConfig(allowlist=CATALOGUE.allowlist, timeout=1.0)

staging = None
try:
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="deploy-") as workdir:
        staging = Path(workdir)
        with scoped(policy):
            python("-c", MIGRATE).run_sync(
                context=ExecutionContext(cwd=staging, cancel_grace=0.5)
            )
except TimeoutExpired as exc:
    print(f"timed out after {exc.timeout}s")
    print(f"partial output: {exc.output!r}")
print(f"cleaned up: {staging is not None and not staging.exists()}")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  {% call ui.output('Observed output', 'stdout') %}
timed out after 1.0s
partial output: 'migrating\n'
cleaned up: True
  {% endcall %}
</div>
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <p>
    {{ api.ref('TimeoutExpired') }} carries what the child had printed
    before its deadline, and the scratch directory was still removed: the
    exception unwound through the <code>with</code> block like any other.
    Every expiry also writes a <code>subprocess_timeout_expired</code>
    warning to the <code>cuprum.timeout</code> logger on stderr, with the
    process ID and the deadline.
  </p>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block policy %}
<p>
  The catalogue lists this interpreter and <code>tar</code>, and the scope's
  allowlist is that catalogue's, so nothing else can run inside the block. The
  overlay, the deadline, and the audit hook apply to every command in the
  scope and disappear when it closes; none of them changes the process's own
  environment.
</p>
{% call ui.callout('preview', 'Forthcoming: removing a variable') %}
<p>
  An overlay can add or change environment variables but not remove one, so a
  step that must run without an inherited variable, a credential say, cannot
  yet say so. Explicit environment replacement and unset are in progress in
  <a href="{{ repository_url }}/pull/466">pull request #466</a>, and are not in
  {{ cuprum_version }}.
</p>
{% endcall %}
{% endblock %}
